## Per-session RAM budget

| Layer | Where | Size | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Kernel pipe | OS | ~64 KB | Drained continuously |
| node-pty handle | Main | negligible | Just an fd wrapper |
| RingBuffer (raw bytes) | Main | **max 5 MB / session** | Drop-oldest; held until PTY dies |
| StateMachine | Main | bytes | Activity tracking |
| xterm scrollback | Renderer | **≈ 16–48 MB / tab** | 50k lines × 80 cols × 12 B; worst case |
| xterm active screen | Renderer | ~rows × cols × 12 B | Negligible (~50 KB) |
| WebglAddon GPU | Renderer | few MB | Glyph atlas + framebuffer |
| terminal-cache | Renderer | holds xterm | Same xterm reused across React mount cycles |

## Scaling

| Scenario | Main RAM | Renderer RAM |
|---|---|---|
| 1 active task, 1 tab | 5 MB | ~20–50 MB |
| 10 tasks open, 1 tab each | 50 MB | 200–500 MB |
| 1 task, 4 split panes | 20 MB | 80–200 MB |

**Inactive tabs stay mounted** (`display: none`) per current architecture — so every opened tab holds its xterm scrollback in RAM until the task tab is fully closed (not just hidden).

## Lifecycle (post-revert)

1. **PTY spawn** → node-pty creates master fd, RingBuffer allocates.
2. **Live output** → bytes flow OS → kernel → pty → RingBuffer (5MB cap, drop-oldest) → IPC → xterm VT parser → screen → eventually scrolls off into 50k line ring.
3. **Tab unmount** → xterm stays in `terminal-cache` (per session). RingBuffer untouched.
4. **Tab remount** → xterm pulled from cache. `getBufferSince(-1)` replays missed chunks from RingBuffer. xterm scrollback intact from prior life.
5. **PTY exit** → RingBuffer freed (after 100 ms grace). xterm in cache holds last view. On next mount: dead-state UI shown.
6. **App restart** → Everything gone. No disk persistence.

## Key invariants (sustainability)

- **xterm = single source of truth for terminal display.** No parallel VT parser anywhere.
- **RingBuffer = transport layer**, not display. Holds raw bytes only for "missed chunks on remount." Bounded at 5 MB regardless of session age.
- **Alt-screen, /resume picker, vim, lazygit** — handled inside xterm's VT parser; never leak into scrollback by construction.
- **No disk I/O on PTY hot path.** Every byte stays in RAM. Predictable performance.

## Decisions this enables

If user wants **less RAM**: lower `terminal_scrollback` default. Each 10k reduction ≈ 4–10 MB / tab.

If user wants **cold-restore back**: add `@xterm/headless` in main, snapshot on unmount. Don't reintroduce raw-byte mirror.

If user wants **bounded total RAM across many tabs**: implement tab eviction (dispose xterm when tab inactive >N min, rebuild from RingBuffer on focus). xterm dispose path already exists in `terminal-cache`.

